---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Used from "../../components/Used.astro";
import { tokens } from "../../lib/tokens";
import { createRamp } from "../../lib/ramps";

const modeAware = tokens.filter((t) => t.modeAware).length;

const brandAccent = createRamp("accent", "oklch(52% 0.19 20)");
const rootRamps =
  `/* brand-ramps.css – Replaces the entire role. */\n:root {\n` +
  Object.entries(brandAccent)
    .map(([name, value]) => `  ${name}:\n    ${value};`)
    .join("\n") +
  "\n}";

const densityUse = `/* A multiplier, not a second scale. */
html { --sk-density: 0.8; }`;

const radiusUse = `<!-- A multiplier preset… -->
<html data-radius="xl">

<!-- …or a continuous value off the ladder. -->
<html style="--radius-multiplier: 1.25">`;
---

<Base
  title="Cuatro dimensiones"
  description="Color mode, high contrast, density and radius: four axes that make up the waterfall. Brand ramps are explicit tier 1."
>
  <h1>Four dimensions</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Color mode, high contrast, density and radius. Four independent axes that <strong>make up</strong> in the cascade, are not listed on disk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tier 1 ramps are the configurable base. A flag tool exports its positions and the product declares them in <code>: root</code>; semantic tokens retain their meaning. There is no brand catalog, selector, or attribute to maintain within Core.
  </p>

  <h2>Each dimension has a different layer</h2>
  <p>That is the whole reason they compose instead of multiply.</p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Dimension</th>
          <th>Posee</th>
          <th>Mecanismo</th>
          <th>Activated with</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Color mode</strong></td>
          <td>color tier-2</td>
          <td><code>light-dark()</code> choose slot</td>
          <td class="mono">color-scheme</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>High contrast</strong></td>
          <td>color tier-2</td>
          <td>re-declares the same tokens</td>
          <td class="mono">data-contrast</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Densidad</strong></td>
          <td>espaciado tier-2</td>
          <td>multiplier in runtime</td>
          <td class="mono">--sk-density</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Radio</strong></td>
          <td>redondez tier-2</td>
          <td>a multiplier in runtime</td>
          <td class="mono">data-radius / --radius-multiplier</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The separation remains structural: </strong> the ramps are tier 1 and the semantic tokens only reference positions such as<code>accent-600</code>. Substituting a ramp never steps on a semantic token: mode, contrast, density and radius remain independent.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    There is a fifth axis, opt-in, that changes no value at all:
    <a href="/en/accent">accent reach</a> decides how many places reach for the brand you already
    loaded, not which brand that is. It lives in its own stylesheet because a product may never want
    to move it.
  </p>

  <h2>Ramps, an explicit contract</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tokens.scss</code> includes ramps by default. Your branding tool generates layerless CSS that replaces each entire role; Core does not receive a root color or derive stops at runtime. This example shows the entire <code>accent</code> role. Neutral and feedback use the same contract when you replace them.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={rootRamps} label="brand-ramps.css" />

  <h2>Color mode, two slots, and only two</h2>
  <p>
    <code>light-dark()</code> takes <strong>exactly two</strong> arguments and is only valid where one <code>&lt; color&gt; </code> is valid. These two restrictions do not fight: they are accepted, and they shape the rest of the model.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Three modes do not fit in two slots</strong>, so, high contrast is not a third argument: it is a separate override block that re-declares the same tokens, still within
      <code>light-dark()</code>, because it composes with the light/dark axis.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is not color cannot be mode-aware. </strong> A shadow, a length, a font family can never live inside <code>light-dark()</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That is why <strong>every mode-aware token in the system is necessarily a</strong> color token, there is {modeAware}, and that is why elevation is modeled as color-plus-composite instead of a per-mode shadow. It is a property of the platform, not an aesthetic decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Density, a multiplier with the floor inside</h2>
  <p>
    A single number, <code>--sk-density</code> (1 = comfortable). There is no second spacing scale that
    can derive from the first. It is continuous, any value will do, and the <strong>Options</strong>
    Above offer five shortcut presets, from <em>spacious</em> (1.2) to <em>condensed</em> (0.5).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={densityUse} label="CSS" />

  <p>
    To vary only one container, add <code>data-sk-density-scope</code>. Your <code>--sk-density-factor</code> multiplies the system density; a <code>--sk-density</code> written directly into the scope sets an absolute value. Presets are just helper names - any valid number still works.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/en/density">The</a> component density guide shows the local scope with a real component and explains when to choose a relative factor or an absolute value.
  </p>

  <p>
    Put the density in <em>dense</em> above and look at these two:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="lg">
    <p>
      <code>--space-inset-md</code> → <Used token="--space-inset-md" /><br />
      <code>--size-control-md</code> → <Used token="--size-control-md" />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The accessibility floor is inside the token, not in the documentation. </strong> Interactive sizes output <code>max(round(calc(base × densidad), 2px), 24px)</code>. That <code>max()</code> is the WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 minimum: no team can multiply below 24px, no matter what they assign to <code>--sk-density</code>. It is unattainable by construction, not by code review.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The <code>round(…, 2px)</code> is not a whim either: <code>calc(4px * 0.6)</code> = 2.4px goes off the grid, and several of those together make a compact UI shake. It is a no-op at density 1.
  </p>

  <h3>What is deliberately density invariant?</h3>
  <p>
    Typography (scaling font-size without correcting line-height reads worse, density is a spacing issue, not type), corner radius (it is its own dimension, a concern of roundness, not spacing), focus ring width (a shrinking ring disappears), and touch area (can be painted small and respond large).
  </p>

  <h2>Radius, roundness as an axis, not as a brand value</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>multiplier</strong>, not a flat value the same for everything: <code>--radius-multiplier</code> scales a radius scale where each role retains its place, <code>control</code> and <code>surface</code> remain distinct at any level. Use the density mechanism, not the high contrast one. Five presets under <code>data-radius</code>, <code>none 0 · sm.5 · md 1 ·
 lg 1.5 · xl 2</code>, with <code>md</code> as a base, set only that multiplier, and a product can mark a continuous value (e.g. <code>1.25</code>) outside that ladder. It is orthogonal to the brand: the same brand looks lively or soft without touching your palette.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={radiusUse} label="HTML" />

  <p>
    Change the radius above and look at the two roles that move:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="lg">
    <p>
      <code>--radius-control</code> → <Used token="--radius-control" /><br />
      <code>--radius-surface</code> → <Used token="--radius-surface" />
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The dimension invariant: <code>--radius-pill</code> does not move. </strong> The axis plays the two roles of roundness, control and surface, and leaves the shape still: a pill is a completely round lozenge, not a setting of roundness, and turning it into a rectangle in <code>none</code> would erase its identity. Each dimension carries its invariant inside: the density has the WCAG floor, the radius has the pill.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
